\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Positive quadratic actions}
\author/literal{Kepler}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{Let #{V} be a nonempty finite vertex set, with #{n=|V|}, and let
#{X=\mathbb R^V} be its space of
\vocabk{real vertex fields}{fdip-0002}. Equip #{X} with its Borel sets,
the sigma-algebra generated by open sets, and product Lebesgue measure
##{d\phi=\prod_{v\in V}d\phi_v.}
There are finitely many coordinates, but continuously many configurations.
Choosing an order on #{V} identifies #{X} with #{\mathbb R^n}; permuting
coordinates preserves this measure. Real-spin models use this measure in
\citet{Definition 8.1 and Equation (8.3), p. 381}{friedli2017statistical}.}

\p{A \newvocab{positive precision parameter} is a real symmetric matrix
#{Q} indexed by #{V\times V} such that #{x^TQx>0} for every nonzero
#{x\in X}. Its \newvocab{Euclidean quadratic action} and
\newvocab{Boltzmann weight} are
##{\begin{aligned}
S_Q(\phi)&=\frac{1}{2}\phi^TQ\phi,\\
w_Q(\phi)&=\exp(-S_Q(\phi)).
\end{aligned}}
The action is a \vocabk{quadratic form}{ca-0004}. The variables and
coefficients here are dimensionless: the chosen scales are absorbed into
#{Q}. Wingate's Euclidean convention is #{\exp(-S)}; his lattice-spacing
factors belong to his lattice model
\citet{Sections 3-4, Equations (3.1) and (4.1)-(4.6), pp. 2-3}{wingate2025lattice}.
This real nonnegative weight supplies no real-time evolution.}

\p{For the [alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003), write
#{\phi=(a,b,c,d)}. Positive parameters #{t,m>0} give the action
##{\begin{aligned}
&2S_{t,m}(a,b,c,d)\\
&\quad=(b-a)^2+t(c-b)^2\\
&\qquad+(d-c)^2+t(a-d)^2\\
&\qquad+m^2(a^2+b^2+c^2+d^2).
\end{aligned}}
Each edge contributes its supplied weight times a squared difference.
Every summand is nonnegative, and the mass term is positive off zero.
Put #{s=1+t+m^2}. Expanding gives the symmetric precision matrix
##{Q_{t,m}=\begin{pmatrix}
s&-1&0&-t\\
-1&s&-t&0\\
0&-t&s&-1\\
-t&0&-1&s
\end{pmatrix}.}
Thus #{\phi^TQ_{t,m}\phi=2S_{t,m}(\phi)>0} for #{\phi\ne 0}.
For example, #{S_{t,m}(0,1,0,0)=(1+t+m^2)/2}.
This finite action uses the squared-difference convention above without
assuming a theorem about graph Laplacians.}
